From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Tue, 12 Jul 1994 09:27:53 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-7-102] emergence defined

Peter McCluskey says, of predicting the behavior of
my digital billiard ball table:

>  I am certain that I could find a method that requires less computation
> than simulation (although it might be much harder to code).

And then, later:

> >    How are we to decide which modeling techniques
> >    are simulations and which aren't?
> 
>  I don't have a precise answer. I would say that if we can find 2 different
> algorithms which produce the same output, then we know the simpler one isn't
> a simulation.

Ah, I see.  The simulation of the billiard ball table is the program
that least efficiently predicts its behavior.  Any other program is
not a simulation.

Note that the original proposed definition of an emergent phenomenon
was "one for which the optimal means of prediction is simulation".
Peter's criterion for a model not being a simulation makes it clear
that either:
 * if "optimal" means in terms of program run time, then we can strengthen
   the definition to "one for which the ONLY means of prediction is
   simulation"
 * otherwise, "optimal" can only refer to the accuracy of the model, and
   for clarity we should say "one for which the most accurate means
   of prediction is simulation"

On the meaning of "optimum":

> The sum of the minimum description lengths of each computation done
> by the simplest Turing machine. This is reportedly machine independent.

Er, I don't follow this.  How do we find the "description length" of
a computation?  I'll guess that something like the number of
instructions executed is what is meant; but what is the "simplest
turing machine?"  Do you mean one with a single internal state?
That won't compute very much.  Maybe you mean the simplest universal
turing machine (though I'm not sure why that's important) -- but
if you are referring to running time on a specific machine, it
seems strange to refer to that time as "machine independent"...

>   It clarifies what discoveries would show that something is not emergent.
> If we have tried every reasonable approach to understanding a phenomenon
> and still can't improve on simulation, then it is reasonable to assume that
> it is emergent.

I think it would help me a great deal if you could give me an example
of a case where there is an improvement on simulation (that is, a
non-emergent phenomenon), so I can better get a sense for what is
and is not a simulation.  I keep thinking of things like "a style
of modeling in which time or space is divided into the smallest
feasible pieces and then predictions are made by updating the
internal states of the pieces based on their interaction with other
individual pieces", but that doesn't seem to cover it.  I'm concerned
that all modeling is fundamentally simulation and would prefer
some kind of guidelines for distinguishing simulations since the
definition radically affects the set of emergent phenomena.

> So it's not everything we might want from a definition. Do
> you have a better one?

Well, I posted a tentative different one, anyway, dealing with
clustering and classifications of observation (which we do
by inventing or modifying vocabulary terms).  It has the advantage
of being widely applicable but the disadvantage of being explicitly
subjective... if I fix that I'll let you know!

>  I suggest that it works well for any phenomenon that can be predicted
> digitally.

Hmm.  Let's take the example of predicting the behavior of computer
programs.  There's no general theory of this, in fact there can't
be one; all we can do is "run it" and see what it does.  However,
in some cases we can predict "theoretically" what a particular program
will do.  The behavior of a program that provably ray-traces the ExI
logo is then not an emergent phenomenon, but the behavior of a similar
program that resists "every reasonable approach" to theorizing _is_ an
emergent phenomenon?  Doesn't that seem like the tail wagging the dog?

derek

