Subject: [#94-7-107] AI Acceleration & Self-Determination

Gregory Sullivan started a thread about possible negative consequences of
AI/Nanotech technological development outstripping human capabilities back on
6/25/94.  I would like to express my concerns also.

Carl Feynman wrote 7/6/94: <But I suspect that it is feasible to develop
machines that think a million times faster than humans.  Such machines could
change the world beyond recognition in a matter of hours.....  Speaking aa a
human being, I find this scenario alarming.>

Me too.

Doug Cutrell wrote 7/10/94: <And if some early AI is better at developing new
self-organizational principles than humans, then we might see the rapid
exponential explosion that Carl Feynman has outlined....>

I also see this race between AI and human understanding of the "physics" of
technological development.  Humans, whether trans-, post- or plain old vanilla,
had better reach the understanding first.  This is not a Red Queen
coevolutionary race, it is a race ending in dominance.  I will not submit to
slavery or destruction from our creations.  Our self-determination is not to be
constrained by godlike AI entities.  We cannot be as the Titans to the
Olympians.  As Goethe puts it in "The Song of the Fates":

    "Let human-kind fear the gods!  In their hands they hold everlasting
  overlordship, and they can use it as they please.
    Let any whom they have exalted fear them doubly!  On clouds and
  precipices thrones stand ready round golden tables.
    If discord arises, the guests are hurled down in scorn and disgrace
  into nethermost night, and there they wait vainly, in bonds and in
  darkness, for justice and judgment.
    But the gods remain everlastingly feasting at golden tables.  They
  stride over mountains from summit to summit: out of deep-down chasms
  the breath of suffocated Titans steams up to their nostrils like
  incense, as delicate vapor.
    These lords turn away their eyes and their blessing from whole races,
  and shun in descendants the sight, the mute speech of an ancestor's
  once-loved features.
    Thus the Fates sang; the aged exile in his dark caverns listens to
  their singing, thinks of his children and grandchildren, and shakes
  his head."

Doug again: <I see there being at least two major camps... those who see AI as
requiring the gradual piecing together of a very complicated system, with many
individually crafted components, and those who see AI as potentially arising
emergently from a much more undifferentiated complex system.>

Between the two camps of people, those who see modular development are the
realists.

