From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 12 Jul 94 17:14:41 EDT
Subject: [#94-7-108] AI Acceleration & Time Preference

Robin Hanson on 6/27/94 posted some very interesting information from Alan
Rogers, "The Evolution of Time Preference by Natural Selection", American
Economic Review, June 1994, V84 N3, pp.460-481.  The question of time preference
or discount factor comes up in an interesting way in Stuart Kauffman's "Origins
of Order" book (borrowing some from Walter Fontana):

"Grammar models [symbol strings acting on other symbol strings] may prove useful
in developing a new class of theories about technological coevolution.  The
behavior of the economy depends on grammar complexity, the diversity of
renewable resources, the discount factor B, and how far into the future the
[individual] looks at each period.  Hence this is a model for economic takeoff.
This behavior suggests that technological diversity is a major factor in
abetting economic growth.

The extent to which the [individual] looks into the future governs whether the
economy grows at all, slowly, or rapidly.  ...The expectation that technological
growth is strongly correlated with the capacity to see its implications is
reasonable.  Finally, growth in diversity of goods does not occur as rapidly if
B is close to 0.  If the consumer places little value on the future, diversity
of goods and services remains small.

If we take grammar models seriously as models of technological evolution, then
the decidability problems in Filigreed Fogs and other objects imply that it may
be logically impossible to deduce that a given good is ultimately producible
from the current technologies.  This implies that markets must be incomplete.
The same failure of decidability may imply that economic agents must logically
be boundedly rational.  ...Markets may not clear."

Robin Hanson wrote 6/28/94: <There is a lot of diversity in memetic
reproduction; while a strong religion might be more like asexual reproduction -
a creature devoted to reproducing one idea in its original form, other memetic
reproduction, can be thought of as *very* sexual.  For example, an intellectual
devoted to a whole constellation of diverse ideas, where no one "descendant" who
carries on those ideas shares more a small fraction of the ideas of that
intellectual.>

I am glad this point came up.  Memetic evolution is a supervening selectional
system with more complicated rules than simple natural selection fitness
measures of "survive and reproduce."  If I may be a bit diffuse, and allow
memetic evolution to include cultural evolution, economic evolution and
technological evolution, then I want to make clear that time preferences arising
from natural evolution will be heavily impacted by supervening time preferences
from memetic evolution.  Predictability of future values becomes extremely
complicated because of this.  Some of Robin's implications of time preferences
based on natural selection can be modified due to memetically based discount
factors.  From Robin's post of 6/27/94:

<B) 15-25 year olds less likely to be receptive to extropian discussions of the
future.>

Not necessarily.  There is a definite excitement factor to boundless expansion,
intelligent technology, etc.  I see powerful reasons for this group to get in on
the ground floor here.

<C) Uploads and AIs will likely reproduce asexually at first.  If evolution
dictates their values, they will likely discount the future at their population
growth rate, which is likely the economy's growth rate.>

An upload *is* an asexual reproduction, it is no longer the original self.  As
the time frames for inventing purposes in life stretch out, the impact of
memetic evolutionary selectional systems will come to outweigh the two purposes
natural selection has provided us.  This makes prognostications on upload values
highly speculative.

<D) Time preferences of non-evolving ordinary humans will likely not be very
responsive to huge increases in the economic growth rate.  If wealth doubles
every year, humans will invest and save like the dickens.>

If Kauffman is right, this puts the cart before the horse.  Time preferences
dictate the economic growth rate, not the other way around.  I do not understand
what a "non-evolving" human is, I think this means more slowly evolving, or
possibly non-culturally evolving.

Steve Witham wrote 6/28/94: <How does awareness of how to deal with the facts of
weapons and war advance?  Is it tied to the other variables by some Darwinian
rule?  Maybe he who adapts fast enough wins, and if nobody adapts, you get a bad
war, slowed economies and slowed tech advancement?>

I think Steve asks good questions here.  I subscribe to Kauffman's point that
the ability to see the consequences of technological development is a major
factor in the speed of this development.  All of our discussions about it will
ultimately help set the pace of development to fit our desires.

Carl Feynman wrote 7/10/94: <How fast a small number of people can advance a
technology depends on how well-developed the technology is.>

Technological development, like brain development, is modular.  Modules
surrounded in all directions by other modules.  How the modules are connected
through regulatory mechanisms (whether you call it "metabolism" for the
internals of biological entities or "market dynamics" for industrial complexes)
is an emergent property of both natural and memetic evolution.  The "physics" of
biology and culture needs to be developed further to make better predictions of
the shape of possible emergent structures as each new module is added to either
biological entities, artificial entities or technologically-based economic
markets.  Whether the group of people making developments is small or large
depends on budget constraints to a large degree.  Certainly every new idea about
science, technology or culture arose in only one mind at a time.  The power of
the individual is supreme, it is the source of everything.

Doug Cutrell wrote 7/10/94: <Some people (e.g. Penrose) have contended that
mathematical "intuition" may be a qualitatively harder task than, say,
mechanical engineering...>

Specialists tend to think their specialty is harder than other specialties.
This is due to their extremely limited vision and experience.  Being a
generalist, I think being a generalist is harder than any specialty.  This is
due to my inability to envision or experience the difficulty of any specialty.

