From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Tue, 12 Jul 1994 17:44:25 -0700
Subject: [#94-7-109] Time Preference

Reilly Jones writes:
>I am glad this point came up.  Memetic evolution is a supervening
>selectional system with more complicated rules than simple natural selection
>fitness measures of "survive and reproduce."  If I may be a bit diffuse, and
>allow memetic evolution to include cultural evolution, economic evolution
>and technological evolution, then I want to make clear that time preferences
>arising from natural evolution will be heavily impacted by supervening time
>preferences from memetic evolution.

All evolution is "natural" -- the distinction is between genetic and memetic
evolution.  Since they are in fact coupled evolutionary systems, they
influence each other, not just memes influencing genes.

>Predictability of future values becomes extremely complicated because of
>this.  Some of Robin's implications of time preferences based on natural
>selection can be modified due to memetically based discount factors.  ...
>
><C) Uploads and AIs will likely reproduce asexually at first.  If evolution
>dictates their values, they will likely discount the future at their 
>population growth rate, which is likely the economy's growth rate.>
>
>An upload *is* an asexual reproduction, it is no longer the original self.
>As the time frames for inventing purposes in life stretch out, the impact of
>memetic evolutionary selectional systems will come to outweigh the two
>purposes natural selection has provided us.  This makes prognostications on
>upload values highly speculative.

I don't understand why you think memetic evolution would dominate upload
evolution.  The phrase "time frames for inventing purposes" is opaque to me.
I argue in my upcoming Extropy article that upload evolution would be strong
and fast, not easily dominated by other coupled processes. 

><D) Time preferences of non-evolving ordinary humans will likely not be very
>responsive to huge increases in the economic growth rate.  If wealth doubles
>every year, humans will invest and save like the dickens.>
>
>If Kauffman is right, this puts the cart before the horse.  Time preferences
>dictate the economic growth rate, not the other way around.  

Then Kauffman is not right.  Time preferences and growth are coupled
together - each can influence the other.

Robin Hanson

