From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Tue, 12 Jul 1994 21:58:07 -0800
Subject: [#94-7-110] Human intelligence augmentation accleration

We have recently been discussing the possibility of a "runaway AI
scenario", whereby each successive generation of AI becomes progressively
better at designing new AI. We then *might* have an exponential explosion
of growth, leading to a so-called future "Singularity" (shades of Vernor
Vinge).

It seems to me equally likely that we might encounter a "runaway human
intelligence augmentation" scenario, which is exactly analogous, but where
each generation represents successive waves of mind-augmentation
technology.

Granted, there is no really good "mind-augmentation" technology to date.
But once we get any at all, we will then have "better than human" level
intelligence, and so the same runaway growth cycle might occur immediately.
All the same arguments, pro and con, seem to apply in this case as well.
There may be others as well. Opinions?

(And yes, I know that computers, and even writing paper or the abacus, can
be considered "mind-augmentation" in some sense, but I am referring to
technology that might cause a significant increase on an IQ test score, for
example.  Or perhaps just improve human memory significantly.)

By the way, can anyone tell me about early printed appearances of the term
"singularity" to describe a future sudden transformation of civilization?
The reason I ask is because I wrote a paper for a Roger Sperry class in
1984 using this terminology.  I thought I was coining this usage at the
time, but I'm curious as to whether I might have been exposed to it somehow
before then.  (Perhaps it is such an obvious notion that it has been
independently suggested countless numbers of times...)

Doug

